  "Question": "If \\(\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt{x}}=3,\\) then \\(x=\\)",
  "Question Type": "text",
  "Choices": {
    "A": "\\(1\\)",
    "B": "\\(\\sqrt7\\)",
    "C": "\\(7\\)",
    "D": "\\(49\\)",
    "E": "\\(121\\)"
  },
  "Choices Type": "text",
  "Correct Answer": "D",
  "Solution": "For the expression to be real, \\(x\\geq0\\). Both sides of the given equation are nonnegative, so squaring removes the outer square root:\n\\[\n2+\\sqrt{x}=9,\n\\qquad\n\\sqrt{x}=7.\n\\]\nBoth sides are again nonnegative, so squaring gives\n\\[\nx=7^2=49.\n\\]\nChecking in the original equation,\n\\[\n\\sqrt{2+\\sqrt{49}}=\\sqrt{2+7}=3.\n\\]\nThus the unique solution is \\(\\boxed{49}\\), choice \\(\\text{D}\\).",
